{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.BlochWignerLobachevsky","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BlochWignerLobachevsky","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.PSeries","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.ClausenFunctionMathlibFiller"],"declarations":32,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.blochWigner_eq_vol_div_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.blochWigner_eq_vol_div_three","kind":"theorem","line":636,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L636-L648","doc":"**The identity in the form the investigation asked for**, conditional on\nthe triangulation input: the per-tetrahedron Bloch–Wigner value is exactly one\nthird of the volume.\n\nThis is `§3b-cond` conditional content, and non-degenerate in the sense of\nitem 5 there: the class supplies the *hypothesis* (three tetrahedra of equal\nshape) and the theorem *derives* the division, rather than carrying\n`D = vol/3` as a field and projecting it out. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FiveTwoTriangulation.vol_eq_three_D","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FiveTwoTriangulation.vol_eq_three_D","kind":"theorem","line":634,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L634-L634","doc":"Three tetrahedra, all of that same shape, so the volume is `3·D(shape)`.\nThis is the geometric input, and it is the whole content. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FiveTwoTriangulation.shape_root","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FiveTwoTriangulation.shape_root","kind":"theorem","line":631,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L631-L631","doc":"The shape is a root of the `5₂` saddle cubic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FiveTwoTriangulation.shape_im_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FiveTwoTriangulation.shape_im_pos","kind":"theorem","line":629,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L629-L629","doc":"The shape lies in the upper half plane — the tetrahedra are\nnon-degenerate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FiveTwoTriangulation.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FiveTwoTriangulation.mk","kind":"ctor","line":626,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L626-L626","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FiveTwoTriangulation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FiveTwoTriangulation","kind":"class","line":584,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L584-L634","doc":"**The open input — the `5₂` triangulation.**\n\nThe `5₂` knot complement admits an ideal triangulation by **three** tetrahedra,\nall of the same shape `y₊` (the upper-half-plane root of the saddle cubic), so\nthat `Vol(5₂) = 3·D(y₊)`.\n\nThis is the actual mathematical content of the identity `D(y₊) = Vol(5₂)/3`.\nIt is a fact of hyperbolic geometry (Thurston's gluing equations plus Mostow\nrigidity), not an algebraic one, and it is far outside Mathlib's reach. The\nnumerical check stands at 20 digits — limited by the `Vol(5₂)` literal, not by\nthe computation — but a numerical check is not a proof.\n\nThe shape and the volume are **parameters**, so the class stays `Prop`-valued\nand carries only hypotheses. There is deliberately **no instance**.\n\n⚠ **\"No instance\" is weaker here than it is for `OhtsukiAsymptotic`, and the\ndifference matters.** This class is *satisfiable*, at a **derived** `vol` — the\nfollowing typechecks (probed 2026-08-31, bean `qou-6x46`; deliberately not\ncommitted as an `instance`):\n\n```\nexample {y : ℂ} (hy : IsOhtsukiShape y) :\n    FiveTwoTriangulation ((starRingEnd ℂ) (1 / y))\n      (3 * BlochWigner_lob ((starRingEnd ℂ) (1 / y))) where\n  shape_im_pos   := (conj_inv_is_five_two_shape hy).2\n  shape_root     := (conj_inv_is_five_two_shape hy).1\n  vol_eq_three_D := rfl\n```\n\nand `FiveTwoOhtsukiBridge.exists_isOhtsukiShape` supplies the `hy`. The first\ntwo fields are already theorems there, and the third *defines* `vol`. So the\nabsence of an instance is not unsatisfiability — it is a refusal to launder the\nname `vol`. Adding that instance would make a downstream conclusion read\n`… → nhds (3·D(y₊))`, which is true content, but it would no longer say **\"the\nhyperbolic volume of the `5₂` complement\"**, because Lean has no definition of\nhyperbolic volume to tie the name to. That is the whole gap, and it is a\ndefinitional one, not an arithmetic one.\n\n`OhtsukiAsymptotic` in `FiveTwoOhtsukiBridge` is a different animal: it carries\ngenuine unproved analysis (steepest descent on the Kashaev invariant), and no\ninstance is constructible at all. Do not describe the two as \"the two remaining\naxioms\" without saying which is which. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.real_shape_has_zero_angles","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.real_shape_has_zero_angles","kind":"theorem","line":565,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L565-L574","doc":"**The real root contributes nothing to `D`.** The Bloch–Wigner function\nvanishes on the reals, so in `Σⱼ D(yⱼ)` only the conjugate pair contributes —\nand those cancel. This is why `Σⱼ D(yⱼ) = 0` is *automatic* for any cubic with\none real root and a conjugate pair, and therefore carries **no** `5₂`-specific\ninformation. Recorded because a previous investigation briefly mistook that\nvanishing for evidence of a torsion class. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.landen_root","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.landen_root","kind":"theorem","line":552,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L552-L563","doc":"**The Landen substitution `z = y/(y−1)` carries the saddle cubic to the\nshape cubic**: if `y ≠ 1` is a root of the saddle cubic, then `z = y/(y−1)` is\na root of the shape cubic. Stated over a field, since the substitution\ninverts. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.fiveTwoShapeCubic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.fiveTwoShapeCubic","kind":"def","line":548,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L548-L550","doc":"The `5₂` shape cubic, the Landen image of the saddle cubic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.fiveTwoSaddleCubic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.fiveTwoSaddleCubic","kind":"def","line":544,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L544-L546","doc":"The `5₂` saddle cubic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.regular_tet_angles_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.regular_tet_angles_sum","kind":"theorem","line":530,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L530-L536","doc":"The **regular** ideal tetrahedron `z = e^{iπ/3}` has all three angles equal\nto `π/3` — the degenerate case where the general formula must reproduce the\ncorpus's figure-8 value. Recorded as the angle statement, which is what\n`tetAngles_sum_eq_pi` constrains; the numerical reconciliation with\n`Cl₂(π/3)` needs `ClausenDuplication` below. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.BlochWigner_lob_real_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BlochWigner_lob_real_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":502,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L502-L528","doc":"**`D` vanishes at a real shape parameter in `(0,1)`.**\n\nThe concrete case behind the removed stub: a real `x` gives a flat tetrahedron,\n`arg x = 0`, `arg(1/(1−x)) = 0` and `arg(1 − 1/x) = π`, so all three Lobachevsky\nterms vanish. Note this is a fact about `D` on the *reals* — it says nothing\nabout `D` in general, and in particular does not conflict with the sharp\nnon-zero value `D(y₊) = Vol(5₂)/3` at the complex `5₂` shape. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.BlochWigner_lob_eq_zero_of_angles","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BlochWigner_lob_eq_zero_of_angles","kind":"theorem","line":481,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L481-L500","doc":"**`D` vanishes on a degenerate tetrahedron.** If every dihedral angle\ncollapses to `0` or `π` — which is exactly what a shape parameter off the upper\nhalf plane does — then `D = 0`, because `Λ` vanishes at both.\n\nThis is the honest form of a fact the corpus previously recorded by *defining*\na symbol named `blochWignerD` to be the constant `0`\n(`QOU/Machinery/QDilogarithm.lean`, removed 2026-08-07, bean `qou-ir1l`). Here\nit is derived from `Lob_zero` and `Lob_pi` instead of asserted, and it says what\nis actually true: the vanishing is a property of degenerate *shapes*, not a\nstatement that Bloch–Wigner is trivial on the substrate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.BlochWigner_lob","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BlochWigner_lob","kind":"def","line":415,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L415-L479","doc":"**The general Bloch–Wigner value at shape `z`**, defined through the\ndihedral angles rather than through a complex dilogarithm:\n\n    D(z) = Λ(arg z) + Λ(arg 1/(1−z)) + Λ(arg(1 − 1/z)).\n\nThis extends `BlochWigner_unit_circle` past `|z| = 1`.\n\n⚠ **THIS IS A DEFINITION, AND IT IS NOT THE STANDARD ONE** (recorded\n2026-08-08 after reading Neumann–Zagier 1985 §3). The literature's Bloch–Wigner\nfunction is\n\n    D(z) = Im Li₂(z) + log|z| · arg(1 − z)          (Neumann–Zagier eq. (39))\n\ni.e. it is built from the **dilogarithm**, not from `Λ`. That the Λ-sum above\nequals it is **Kummer's identity** (Lewin, *Polylogarithms*, p. 121 eq. (5.5)),\nwhich Neumann–Zagier invoke explicitly to pass from their eq. (37)\n`Vol(S(z)) = Λ(α)+Λ(β)+Λ(γ)` to eq. (38) `Vol(S(z)) = D(z)`. It is a **theorem,\nnot a definitional unfolding**.\n\nSo, precisely:\n\n* ⚠⚠ **STATUS: KUMMER IS NOW PROVED (2026-08-09, bean `qou-kmmr`).**\n\n  `QOU/Knot/KummerRadial.lean` — `kummer` — proves, sorry-free and on the\n  standard axioms, for every `z` with `Im z > 0`:\n\n      BlochWigner_lob z = Im (cLiTwoExt z) + log‖z‖ · arg(1 − z)\n\n  So the two objects named `D` in this corpus are the **same function** on the\n  upper half plane. This bullet previously said the identity \"cannot currently be\n  proved in this corpus\" (Mathlib has no dilogarithm); that was corrected first to\n  \"unblocked, not impossible\" once `QOU.MathlibExt.cLiTwoExt` was recognised, and\n  is now simply **done**.\n\n  The proof chain, all sorry-free: `KummerUnitCircle` (the Λ-side on `|z| = 1`,\n  which is Clausen duplication) → `KummerDilogHalf` (the `cLiTwoExt` side there,\n  by same-derivative with basepoint `θ = π`) → `IdealTetLawOfSines`\n  (`‖z‖ = sin θ₂/sin θ₃`) → `KummerRadial` (a radial reduction, using the unit\n  circle as basepoint).\n\n* **The prohibition below is therefore LIFTED.** It used to read: \"do not\n  transport dilogarithm facts onto this symbol — the five-term relation, the\n  functional equations, or any N–Z result stated for *their* `D` — without first\n  proving Kummer's identity. The name is shared; the objects are so far only\n  numerically observed to agree.\"\n\n  That condition is now met. Dilogarithm facts about `cLiTwoExt` **may** be\n  transported onto `BlochWigner_lob` on `{Im z > 0}`, via `kummer`. Outside the\n  upper half plane nothing is claimed — `BlochWigner_lob` is built from `arg`s and\n  this file separately proves it vanishes on real shapes in `(0,1)`, which is a\n  branch statement, not an instance of Kummer.\n\n* The numerical cross-check that used to be the only evidence is retained as a\n  control, not as grounds: `computations/qbeta/xi_classical_limit_probe.py`\n  matches the two forms at the substrate anchor to ~1e-6 series accuracy, and\n  Routes 72–73 match them to `1e-41`/`1e-45` at five shapes including `y₁`.\n\nConsequence for `qou-idvt` T2 / `qou-5gai` H4: the target factors as\n(geometric) `Vol = D` ∘ (analytic) `D = Λ-sum`. **The analytic factor is now\nclosed** (`kummer`). The *geometric* one — that a particular triangulation's\nΛ-sum is the hyperbolic volume of a particular knot complement — is untouched and\nis still the whole of H4. Nothing here computes `Vol(5₂)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.tetAngles_sum_eq_pi","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.tetAngles_sum_eq_pi","kind":"theorem","line":357,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L357-L408","doc":"**The dihedral angles of an ideal tetrahedron sum to `π`.**\n\nFor `Im z > 0`,  `arg z + arg(1/(1−z)) + arg(1 − 1/z) = π`.\n\nThe three shapes multiply to `−1`, so the arg-sum is `π` modulo `2π`; each arg\nlies in `(0, π)`, so the sum lies in `(0, 3π)`; the only value in that range\ncongruent to `π` is `π` itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.tet_shape_prod","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.tet_shape_prod","kind":"theorem","line":350,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L350-L355","doc":"**The three angles multiply their shapes to `−1`.**\n`z · (1/(1−z)) · (1 − 1/z) = −1`. Pure algebra, and the source of the `π`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.arg_mem_Ioo_of_im_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.arg_mem_Ioo_of_im_pos","kind":"theorem","line":335,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L335-L348","doc":"For `Im w > 0` the argument lies in the **open** interval `(0, π)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.tet_im_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.tet_im_pos","kind":"theorem","line":311,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L311-L333","doc":"The three Möbius images have **positive imaginary part** whenever `z` does:\nthe upper half plane is preserved. This is what makes them genuine angles. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.tetAngle₃","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.tetAngle₃","kind":"def","line":308,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L308-L309","doc":"Third dihedral angle: the shape parameter seen from the last vertex. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.tetAngle₂","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.tetAngle₂","kind":"def","line":305,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L305-L306","doc":"Second dihedral angle: the shape parameter seen from the next vertex. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.tetAngle₁","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.tetAngle₁","kind":"def","line":302,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L302-L303","doc":"First dihedral angle of the ideal tetrahedron of shape `z`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.Lob_pi","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.Lob_pi","kind":"theorem","line":289,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L289-L294","doc":"`Λ(π) = 0`, since `Cl₂(2π) = 0`. Together with `Lob_zero` this is what makes\n`D` vanish on degenerate (real-shape) tetrahedra. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.Lob_pi_div_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.Lob_pi_div_two","kind":"theorem","line":283,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L283-L287","doc":"`Λ(π/2) = 0`, since `Cl₂(π) = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.Lob_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.Lob_zero","kind":"theorem","line":279,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L279-L281","doc":"`Λ(0) = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.Lob_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.Lob_neg","kind":"theorem","line":273,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L273-L277","doc":"`Λ` is odd, inherited from `Cl₂`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.Lob","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.Lob","kind":"def","line":267,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L267-L271","doc":"The **Lobachevsky function** `Λ(t) = Cl₂(2t)/2`, the standard normalisation\nin which an ideal tetrahedron of dihedral angles `α, β, γ` has volume\n`Λ(α) + Λ(β) + Λ(γ)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.three_Lob_pi_div_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.three_Lob_pi_div_three","kind":"theorem","line":261,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L261-L265","doc":"The regular ideal tetrahedron's Lobachevsky value **is** the figure-8\nper-tetrahedron Clausen value: `3·Λ(π/3) = Cl₂(π/3)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.Cl_2_two_pi_div_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.Cl_2_two_pi_div_three","kind":"theorem","line":246,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L246-L259","doc":"**Consistency with the corpus's figure-8 value.** The regular ideal\ntetrahedron has all three angles `π/3`, so the general formula gives\n`3·Λ(π/3) = 3·Cl₂(2π/3)/2`, and duplication at `θ = π/3` forces\n`Cl₂(2π/3) = (2/3)·Cl₂(π/3)`, returning exactly `Cl₂(π/3)` — the\nper-tetrahedron value behind `Vol(4₁) = 2·Cl₂(π/3)`.\n\nThis is the check that was missing: it is *not* `rfl`, and it validates the\nnon-unit-circle definition against a case the corpus already trusts. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.Cl_2_duplication","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.Cl_2_duplication","kind":"theorem","line":213,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L213-L244","doc":"**The Clausen duplication formula.**\n\n    Cl₂(2θ) = 2·Cl₂(θ) − 2·Cl₂(π − θ)\n\nProved from the Fourier series by the parity split described above. This is\nwhat `ClausenDuplication` was isolating; with it in hand the general\n`BlochWigner_lob` can be checked against the corpus's own unit-circle knots\nrather than only against numerics. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.Cl_2_add_two_pi","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.Cl_2_add_two_pi","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L142-L151","doc":"**`Cl₂` is `2π`-periodic.** Each term has period `2π` in `θ` because\n`sin` is `2π`-periodic and the frequency `k+1` is an integer. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.Cl_2_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.Cl_2_neg","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L134-L140","doc":"**`Cl₂` is odd.** Termwise from `sin(−x) = −sin x`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.summable_clausen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.summable_clausen","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochWignerLobachevsky.lean#L118-L132","doc":"**The Clausen series is summable**, by comparison with `∑ 1/(k+1)²`.\n`|sin x| ≤ 1` is the only analytic input. ","signatureChunk":0}]}